//BUG="a field of a temporary is treated as addressable, so the generated C does not compile"
//OUT="1"

// `size` takes `self` by reference, so the receiver `make().items` has to be
// referenced. `is_expression_physical` (src/compiler/normalizer/reference_check.rs)
// decides whether an expression already has an address or must first be
// spilled into a hidden variable, and it answers "physical" for every
// `FieldAccess` without looking at what the field is read from. Here it is
// read from a call result, which has no address, so codegen emits
//
//     func25(func24((&func1().F0)))
//
// and gcc rejects it: "lvalue required as unary '&' operand". The compiler
// itself reports success, so the user is left with a C error in a file they
// did not write.
//
// Taking the reference of the call result directly works — it is spilled
// (05_functions/44_a_method_on_a_call_result.cpl), and so does a field of a
// struct literal, because a C compound literal happens to be an lvalue
// (08_structs/34). Only the combination fails.
//
// Everything that needs the address of such a field breaks the same way:
// `make().x += 5` ("lvalue required as unary '&' operand" again) and the
// plain assignment `make().x = 5` ("lvalue required as left operand of
// assignment"), which the "Left hand side is non-assignable." check lets
// through for the same reason.
//
// The fix is for a field access to be exactly as physical as the expression
// it is applied to.

struct Wrapper
    items: Vec[i32]

fn make
    w = Wrapper items Vec[i32]()
    w.items.push(1)
    ret w

fn main
    out "{make().items.size()}"
